  {
    "episode_id": 92309,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/2/92309.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 19,
    "turn_text": "Oh absolutely, so my parents were on the board at the Cass County Farm Bureau for many years and as a child my sister and I every month would go with our folks to the meetings and we would play out in the offices while with other children of other board members while those meetings went on and grew up, you know, being involved in understanding how important Missouri Farm Bureau is to agriculture to have a say because there's not that many of us who live and have the privilege of living on a farm who have that understanding perspective of agriculture and it's very important that we speak with one voice and pool our resources together to advocate for those things. So when my husband and I met at Mizzou and then got married later, one of the first things we did was join Farm Bureau and got involved in that. where my dad was president for a while when I was a kid, then my husband Lowell was asked to be president for a few years, and then after I got out of the legislature, became a mom and decided to spend some years back focusing on full-time as on our family and our farm. I was asked to be the county president as well, served for three years as the Cass County president of the Missouri Farm Bureau and of course got the opportunity to be a part of the resolutions process and all the things that are going on right here every year at the Missouri Farm Bureau Annual Meeting and we were also asked to be on the Young Farmer and Ranchers Committee back when we were young. which was a wonderful experience. And then Lowell was asked to be the state chairman of that and got to serve as a member of the state Farm Bureau Board as a young farmer representative. And so, yes, I do have a lot of background. And I don't consider Farm Bureau an organization. I consider it family. I really do because Farm Bureau is a family. You get to meet people that you have those friendships for life. You share the same values. You're in the fight together and you love the same things. You love your country and you love rural America.",
