  {
    "episode_id": 92309,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/2/92309.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 49,
    "turn_text": "to do with farming, is the nutrition programs. The old food stamps program now called SNAP. the women's infants and children, those other nutrition assistance programs that comprise 80% of all the dollars that go out. I think we've had, I'm on a nutrition subcommittee for the Agriculture Committee and we've been exploring what is working, what isn't working with those programs. And there's going to be efforts to try to make sure that money is well spent and goes to people who truly need a hand You know, a temporary hand up and make sure there's not fraud in it. There probably be some proposals dealing with work requirements for able-bodied adults who are participating in that program. So we're going to see some changes, have some important discussions there. But in my mind, this number one priority is still to keep crop insurance. because it is still the most valued part of the Farm Bill based on all my discussions with farmers I visit with. It is a very important risk management tool. It's also important to the banks and to the creditors and stuff to have that assurance there. So there's probably going to be some changes proposed there, I put a means testing or some other provisions, I'm going to oppose those, probably push back on those. But we're going to be looking at rural development and other areas would be rural broadband. And I've got some a bill that I'm going to be trying to work into the CRP section. So there's a lot of little changes that'll be there. But I think it's overall going to stay the same basic structure.",
