    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/2/92309.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 58,
    "turn_text": "Right. Well, I'm excited about it's the CRP, a grazing flexibility act. And during the contract for CRP midway through that contract was a 10 year contract or whatever, there's a requirement that the land be cleared in some way. They're through burning or chemical burning or through disking. And the landowner gets some cost share from the government, from the taxpayer in order to help pay for that. It's just good for the land to do that. Well, my bill will allow grazing as an option. And it's a win-win for everybody because one is win for the taxpayer. In exchange for that, there will be no cost share that goes out for doing that. But it's also good for wildlife. quails forever, pheasants forever, turkey, federation, nature, conservancy. They're all supportive of this bill because just having cows graze down the grass is preferable than actually the burning and the chemical burning. And plus it's a win for the cattle rancher as well. So no cost to the taxpayer, win, win. I'm excited about it and think even though I introduced it as a stand on legislation, my goal is as soon as we start discussing this to roll it into the farm bill.",
