  {
    "episode_id": 92309,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/2/92309.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 62,
    "turn_text": "They just are concerned about trade. The prices of the commodities are low. We have a large supply, which that's good. We've had some good yields. But we've got to sell our crops abroad to 90% of where the population is. And the TPP that we were going to go into was expected to bring $5.5 billion more dollars every year to the farm economy. I understand the president had some concerns about that other areas maybe weren't preferential for our country. But he has pledged to do very aggressive bilateral trade agreements. And anytime I have a chance to talk with anyone at the administration, that is what I certainly try to emphasize is that as quickly as possible, we've got to get this trade. These trade agreements going because China is now trying to go around us. That was one of the benefits of the TPP is that it shut China out. Now they're unilaterally talking to all these other people trying to get into what's left at the TPP. And we just can't wait. We've got to be aggressive on that front. And I think I hear a lot about that back home. We've got to increase our trade. And in the farm bill, we'll be able to help some. There are provisions that allow for some market access and some promotion. And I'm very supportive of those as well to give us locally the tools to go sell our products and to increase trade here in the fourth district.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
